  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The horn-of-plenty, also known as the black trumpet, is a funnel-shaped fungus with a hollow interior and a dark grayish-black color. Despite its dark appearance, it is a highly prized edible mushroom known for its rich, earthy flavor and delicate texture.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Funnel Shape",
      "description": "Distinctive trumpet or cornucopia shape with a hollow center running from cap to base",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Ridge-like Ridges",
      "description": "Features shallow, blunt ridges or wrinkles running down the outer surface rather than true gills",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark Coloration",
      "description": "Ranges from dark gray to black with a thin, delicate flesh that is lighter inside",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Horn-of-plenty mushrooms grow on moist forest floors among leaf litter in deciduous and mixed woodlands throughout New England, typically fruiting in summer through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their dark, almost charcoal-like appearance, horn-of-plenty mushrooms are often called 'poor man's truffle' because they have an intensely aromatic, complex flavor similar to true truffles but are much more affordable and accessible to foragers.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
